(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antivirals-quiz"]={"title":"Antivirals Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does valacyclovir achieve substantially higher oral bioavailability than acyclovir?","choices":["It is given intravenously rather than orally","It resists degradation by stomach acid where acyclovir does not","It is absorbed through a different segment of the bowel","It is a prodrug, which is converted after absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The same prodrug strategy explains famciclovir against penciclovir, and valganciclovir against ganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Within what window must oseltamivir be started?","choices":["As soon as possible, within 48 hours of symptom onset","Within 7 days of symptom onset","Within the first 24 hours, as with acyclovir in chickenpox","At any point during the illness, with equal benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because it blocks release rather than killing virus already present, late treatment has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"What proportion of patients must stop intravenous ganciclovir because of side effects?","choices":["About a third","About 1 in 20","Almost none; it is generally well tolerated","Essentially all patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a striking figure, and the reason the better-absorbed oral prodrug matters clinically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"What happens to newly formed influenza virions when neuraminidase is inhibited?","choices":["They cannot uncoat once inside the next cell","Their nucleic acid cannot be replicated","They cannot be cleaved free of the host cell as budding progeny","They can no longer attach to the receptor on the next host cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they remain tethered at the surface, so the infection cannot spread to new cells. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Herpes simplex encephalitis. Which type affects the brain?","choices":["Type 2","Both types equally","Neither; encephalitis is a cytomegalovirus manifestation","Type 1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — type 1 covers mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which infections are acyclovir and valacyclovir indicated for?","choices":["Cytomegalovirus retinitis in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B","Human immunodeficiency virus infection","Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cytomegalovirus requires ganciclovir or valganciclovir instead. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which herpesviruses are grouped as targets for antiviral therapy?","choices":["Herpes simplex and influenza A only","Cytomegalovirus and human immunodeficiency virus","Varicella-zoster and hepatitis B","Herpes simplex, varicella-zoster and cytomegalovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the first two share the acyclovir family, while cytomegalovirus needs ganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for acyclovir?","choices":["Seizures, delirium and tremor","Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Peripheral neuropathy and tendon rupture","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A patient presents on day 4 of influenza. What limits oseltamivir?","choices":["Acyclovir should be substituted","The 48-hour window for starting treatment has passed","Treatment should be started at a doubled dose to compensate","Treatment is equally effective at any point in the illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the drug blocks release of new virus rather than clearing existing virus, starting late has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which of penciclovir and famciclovir is the oral prodrug?","choices":["Neither is a prodrug; both are given intravenously","Famciclovir, which is oral and considerably better absorbed","Penciclovir, which is oral and considerably better absorbed","Both are prodrugs of acyclovir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penciclovir is the intravenous parent with poor oral bioavailability. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does oseltamivir represent?","choices":["Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Stimulating the host immune system","Inhibiting viral release","Blocking viral attachment to cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — neuraminidase frees budding progeny, so blocking it traps new virus at the cell surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What kind of compound is oseltamivir as administered?","choices":["A live attenuated preparation","A prodrug converted to its carboxylate form","An active guanine analogue requiring no conversion","A monoclonal antibody against viral haemagglutinin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — another prodrug in a lecture full of them, alongside valacyclovir, famciclovir and valganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which sites does herpes simplex type 2 characteristically affect?","choices":["Liver, spleen and bone marrow","Genitals, rectum, hands or meninges","Mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain","Retinitis, oesophagitis and colitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, against type 1's mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is happening to oseltamivir resistance?","choices":["It has been becoming a problem over time","Resistance has never been reported","Resistance is prevented entirely by starting within 48 hours","Resistance occurs only in influenza B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same trajectory the antibacterial section describes for the fluoroquinolones. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which two antivirals are given intravenously as the parent compound?","choices":["Oseltamivir and acyclovir, neither having an oral form","Valganciclovir and valacyclovir, given intravenously in transplant patients","Penciclovir and ganciclovir, each with an oral prodrug available","Valacyclovir and famciclovir, each with an intravenous prodrug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — famciclovir and valganciclovir respectively are the better-absorbed oral forms. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for ganciclovir?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy and tendon injury","Blurred vision and mental confusion from enzyme induction","Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Seizures, delirium and tremor only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, occurring in a minority of patients alongside the haematologic effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Why is influenza treatment framed around a season?","choices":["Flu season runs roughly October to March, which is when the illness is circulating","The drug is only manufactured and distributed during those particular months","Resistance disappears outside the season","Renal dosing requirements change with the season"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it frames when an influenza-like illness should raise suspicion in the first place. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which set of antivirals are prodrugs?","choices":["Acyclovir, penciclovir, ganciclovir and zanamivir","Amphotericin B, caspofungin and griseofulvin","Metronidazole, linezolid and daptomycin","Valacyclovir, famciclovir, valganciclovir and oseltamivir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Improving oral absorption is a recurring design theme across the whole antiviral section. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which manifestations does cytomegalovirus cause?","choices":["Influenza-like illness with fever and myalgia","Retinitis, oesophagitis and colitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they occur chiefly in transplant recipients and other immunocompromised patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which antiviral does NOT act on viral nucleic acid?","choices":["Ganciclovir, which inhibits in a manner similar to acyclovir","Penciclovir, which has a mechanism similar to acyclovir","Oseltamivir, which inhibits neuraminidase to block viral release","Acyclovir, which is incorporated into viral DNA"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The guanine analogues all terminate the nucleic acid chain; oseltamivir works at a completely different stage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"}]};
